Passing a string as the second parameter does not currently work for `get()`.

<blockquote>Warning (4096): Argument 2 passed to Cake\Network\Http\Client::get() must be of the type array, string given, called in /home/vagrant/inventory2/plugins/CakeREST/src/REST/API.php on line 79 and defined [CORE/src/Network/Http/Client.php, line 193]<br/>
Warning (2): http_build_query() [function.http-build-query]: Parameter 1 expected to be Array or Object.  Incorrect value given [CORE/src/Network/Http/Client.php, line 351]</blockquote>

The [documentation](http://book.cakephp.org/3.0/en/core-libraries/httpclient.html) states:

<blockquote>The type key can either be a one of ‘json’, ‘xml’ or a full mime type. When using the type option, you should provide the data as a string.</blockquote>

However, setting `type` to `x-www-form-urlencoded` results in an "Unknown type alias" error, so I have to specify Content-type explicitly. Here's the workaround that's required in Cake 3.0.4:

```
// Have to use http_build_query to bypass security vulnerability - see:
// https://github.com/cakephp/cakephp/issues/6540
$http = new Client();

if ($method == 'get') {
    $response = $http->$method(
        "$url?" . http_build_query($arguments),
        [],
        [
            'headers' => $headers,
        ]
    );
} else {
    $headers['Content-type'] = 'x-www-form-urlencoded';
    $response = $http->$method(
        $url,
        http_build_query($arguments),
        [
            'headers' => $headers,
        ]
    );
}
```

It would actually be faster to use `file_get_contents()` with `stream_context_create()`.
